    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Let's reason through the problem step by step:\n\n1. We start with 10 club members, consisting of 5 boys and 5 girls.\n2. The first member chosen is a girl.\n3. The probability of the first member being a girl is 5 out of the total 10 members. So, P(girl first) = 5/10 = 1/2.\n4. After one girl is chosen, there are now 9 members left in the club, with only 4 girls remaining.\n5. The probability of the next member being another girl is 4 out of the remaining 9 members. So, P(girl second | before girl first) = 4/9.\n6. To find the overall probability of both events happening (choosing two girls in a row), we multiply the probabilities of each individual event:\n   P(girl girls) = P(girl first) × P(girl second | before girl first) = (1/2) × (4/9)\n\nBy multiplying these probabilities, we get:\n\nP(girl girls) = (1 * 4) / (2 * 9)\n               = 4/18\n               = 2/9\n\nSo, the probability that both members chosen are girls is **2/9** or approximately 22.2% chance.",
